{
 "id": "2019-kang-urban",
 "name": "Urban vs Green Landscapes: Restorative Effects (rating)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Kang, Y., & Kim, E. J. (2019). Differences of restorative effects while viewing urban landscapes and green landscapes. Sustainability 11:2129. Participants view 25 photographs of European landscapes, one at a time in random order: natural scenes in close view (NC1–7) and distant view (ND1–6), and built scenes in close view (BC1–7) and distant view (BD1–5). Each photograph is shown alone for 8 seconds, then rated on 7-point scales for visual aesthetics (very unpleasing – very pleasing), complexity (very uncomplicated – very complicated) and the five-item short Perceived Restorativeness Scale (being away, fascination, coherence, scope, compatibility; very disagree – very agree), with as much time as needed. Gender, age and ethnic background are asked at the end. Analysis as in the paper: PRS = mean of the five items; compare the four landscape types (MANOVA with Tukey post hoc) and correlate aesthetics, complexity and PRS (Pearson). Original result (31 participants): natural scenes were more restorative than built scenes regardless of distance; natural distant views scored highest on PRS (4.7/7), built distant views lowest (3.9); aesthetics correlated positively with PRS (r = 0.483) and complexity slightly negatively (r = −0.168).\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the original was a lab study with an eye tracker (fixations, scan path); eye movements are not recorded here. (2) The original used two fixed slideshow orders; here each participant gets their own random order. (3) The paper gives only the anchors for aesthetics and complexity, so the question wording \"How visually pleasing / How complicated is this landscape?\" is ours. (4) \"Other / prefer not to say\" options were added to gender and ethnic background.\n\nPhotographs: Figure A1 of the paper (CC BY 4.0), used as published with credit to the authors; the template is removed if the authors object.",
